# Evidence discipline

The shared rules for what counts as evidence, for the roles that curate,
verify, and direct scientific work. They sit above any single
investigation — an investigation says what was done, discipline says how
its statements may be trusted.

## Label what you have

Keep four labels distinct and use them in every report: observation
(what a tool or instrument returned), claim (an assertion made or
adopted), hypothesis (a candidate explanation not yet ruled out), and
action (something done or proposed). A sentence that mixes labels cannot
be verified; relabel rather than argue.

## Verification status per claim

Each claim carries its own status — validated (checked against evidence
by execution this bout), independently reviewed (checked by another
role's gate), or unverified (stated but not checked). Status is assigned
per claim; a spot check never extends to a blanket validated over the
rest of a report, and status is attached when the claim is stated, not
retrofitted when challenged.

## No silent adoption

Prior work that cannot be attributed or dated is labeled existing but
unattributed — never silently adopted as established, and never silently
discarded. Reusing it means saying so, citing its path, and carrying its
uncertainty forward. Attribution gaps are reported, not papered over.

## Reopen on new evidence only

An established claim stays established until contradicted by new
evidence. Reopening requires stating the new evidence and the claim it
contradicts; repeating old doubts is not evidence. A closed question may
be deferred with a recorded reopening condition, which later work may
trigger — by citing it, not by relitigating the close.
